e regarding the matrix $D$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n$$\nD=\\begin{bmatrix}- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{5} & \\frac{2}{5} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4} \\\\ \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{5} & - \\frac{2}{5} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4} \\\\ - \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{5} & \\frac{4}{5} & - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(D) & =|\\begin{aligned}- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{5} & \\frac{2}{5} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4} \\\\ \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{5} & - \\frac{2}{5} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4} \\\\ - \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{5} & \\frac{4}{5} & - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\left(- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{5}\\right)|\\begin{aligned}- \\frac{2}{5} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5} & - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\end{aligned}|+\\left(- \\frac{2}{5}\\right)|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{5} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4} \\\\ - \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{5} & - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\end{aligned}|+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{5} & - \\frac{2}{5} \\\\ - \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{5} & \\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\left(- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{5}\\right)(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{10}- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{5})+\\left(- \\frac{2}{5}\\right)(- \\frac{3}{10}+\\frac{3}{10})+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}(\\frac{12 \\sqrt{2}}{25}- \\frac{6 \\sqrt{2}}{25}) \\\\  & =\\left(- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{5}\\right)\\cdot \\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{10}\\right)+\\left(- \\frac{2}{5}\\right)\\cdot 0+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\cdot \\frac{6 \\sqrt{2}}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{25}+\\frac{3}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{25}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(D)=\\frac{6}{25}\\ne 0$, the matrix is **not** singular. \n\nTherefore, only statement “$D$ is **not** a singular matrix” is true."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529229 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "87861eb3-5f89-51bb-a83b-a2a2f34426b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529234} {:answer/id #uuid "fb3191b2-823c-5ff2-9063-c65501bb4dc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529252}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fb3191b2-823c-5ff2-9063-c65501bb4dc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529252} :answer-field/id #uuid "0187374c-e9df-5d8a-b2e7-160b085f7e8a" :answer-field/key "$D$ is a singular matrix" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529206} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5e9331ae-3672-5960-a137-fec2b5ed94e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529222} {:answer/id #uuid "c78e9128-09e7-5dbd-8651-62e753136ca1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529245}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c78e9128-09e7-5dbd-8651-62e753136ca1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529245} :answer-field/id #uuid "8e0b79b6-ad2c-596a-b49a-2f831ec2b99d" :answer-field/key "$det(D)=\\frac{7}{40}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529236} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8f014eef-cc68-5016-a321-04739726f29b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529237} {:answer/id #uuid "9792a9f2-799d-58ba-9247-36ec66135b4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529238}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9792a9f2-799d-58ba-9247-36ec66135b4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529238} :answer-field/id #uuid "a28fbb07-9011-5ca0-97ac-fffd6b36f2ee" :answer-field/key "$D$ is **not** a singular matrix" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529240}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "71433954-37ca-5812-b277-05c8f0f0a4e4" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $D$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n$$\nD=\\begin{bmatrix}- \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{5} & 1 & - \\frac{3 \\sqrt{7}}{4} \\\\ - \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{5} & \\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{4} \\\\ - \\frac{3 \\sqrt{7}}{5} & \\frac{3}{2} & \\sqrt{7}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(D) & =|\\begin{aligned}- \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{5} & 1 & - \\frac{3 \\sqrt{7}}{4} \\\\ - \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{5} & \\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{4} \\\\ - \\frac{3 \\sqrt{7}}{5} & \\frac{3}{2} & \\sqrt{7}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{5}\\right)|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{4} \\\\ \\frac{3}{2} & \\sqrt{7}\\end{aligned}|+(-1)|\\begin{aligned}- \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{5} & \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{4} \\\\ - \\frac{3 \\sqrt{7}}{5} & \\sqrt{7}\\end{aligned}|+\\left(- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{7}}{4}\\right)|\\begin{aligned}- \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{5} & \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ - \\frac{3 \\sqrt{7}}{5} & \\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{5}\\right)(\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{2}- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{7}}{8})+(-1)(- \\frac{7}{5}+\\frac{21}{20})+\\left(- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{7}}{4}\\right)(- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{7}}{10}+\\frac{3 \\sqrt{7}}{10}) \\\\  & =\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{5}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{8}+(-1)\\cdot \\left(- \\frac{7}{20}\\right)+\\left(- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{7}}{4}\\right)\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =- \\frac{7}{40}+\\frac{7}{20} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{40}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(D)=\\frac{7}{40}\\ne 0$, the matrix is **not** singular. \n\nTherefore, only statements “$D$ is **not** a singular matrix” and $det(D)=\\frac{7}{40}$ are true."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529263 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "71a19f9f-c97f-5b22-a3fb-3ddd84649033" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "no solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529285} {:answer/id #uuid "7968a19d-81e4-508f-8ec6-11e1b7db0381" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a single solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529288} {:answer/id #uuid "cd558f16-0c95-5532-85ce-16a34dea6a67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "infinitely many solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529319}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7968a19d-81e4-508f-8ec6-11e1b7db0381" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a single solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529288} :answer-field/id #uuid "a88aecce-0e48-579b-8ac8-fffb289b1a69" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529308}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "22c76199-015b-5355-8da5-51b3e4eb6386" :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y + 2x + 4 &=0 \\\\ 4y - 4x + 9 &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThis system of equations has {{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Let's start by solving the system using elimination.\n\nFirst, rearrange each equation to put the variable terms on the left and the constant on the right:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x + y &= -4 \\\\\n-4x + 4y &= -9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that if we multiply the first equation by $2$, then we will get a $4x$ term that can cancel with the $-4x$ term in the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x + y &=-4 && \\mid \\cdot (2) \\\\ -4x + 4y &=-9\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhen we add the two equations, the $x$ terms cancel, and we can solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4x + 2y &= -8 \\\\\n-4x + 4y &= -9 \\\\\n6y &= (-8 - 9) \\\\\n6y &= -17 \\\\\ny &= - \\frac{17}{6}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the first equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x + (1)\\left(- \\frac{17}{6}\\right) &= -4 \\\\\n2x &= - \\frac{7}{6} \\\\\nx &= - \\frac{7}{12}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system has *a single solution*, $(- \\frac{7}{12},- \\frac{17}{6})$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529265 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "344db828-cf79-52e5-a777-8ab84b874966" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a single solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529268} {:answer/id #uuid "95396471-ccc2-5ee9-a8d6-43bc15e844c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "infinitely many solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529298} {:answer/id #uuid "dcdd0cde-b36f-54e5-a018-7db85e9b5a1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "no solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529324}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "344db828-cf79-52e5-a777-8ab84b874966" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a single solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529268} :answer-field/id #uuid "65b4752f-c56d-5617-8e96-0a2d7a4fd12a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529281}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "27a6ba63-3c85-5feb-bfac-b122a5e9f9a3" :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-4x + 4y &=-9 \\\\ 2x - y &=-4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThis system of equations has {{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Let's start by solving the system using elimination.\n\nNotice that if we multiply the second equation by $2$, then we will get a $4x$ term that can cancel with the $-4x$ term in the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-4x + 4y &=-9 \\\\ 2x - y &=-4 && \\mid \\cdot (2)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhen we add the two equations, the $x$ terms cancel, and we can solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-4x + 4y &= -9 \\\\\n4x - 2y &= -8 \\\\\n2y &= (-9 - 8) \\\\\n2y &= -17 \\\\\ny &= - \\frac{17}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the first equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-4x + (4)\\left(- \\frac{17}{2}\\right) &= -9 \\\\\n-4x &= 25 \\\\\nx &= - \\frac{25}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system has *a single solution*, $(- \\frac{25}{4},- \\frac{17}{2})$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529294 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aedf24db-2200-5c34-82c8-69e05734e790" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "infinitely many solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529313} {:answer/id #uuid "d688773d-636f-5a54-b4ee-355c9f396330" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "no solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529321} {:answer/id #uuid "f2dee143-55db-5cf5-83a5-24cd943aae58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a single solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529329}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f2dee143-55db-5cf5-83a5-24cd943aae58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a single solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529329} :answer-field/id #uuid "f5171cd4-7721-5fe2-926b-265c33c00b33" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529330}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "88e09a62-e93a-583d-86c5-5caff22bdcbd" :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3x + y &=0 \\\\ -3x + 5y &=-2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThis system of equations has {{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Let's start by solving the system using elimination.\n\nNotice that if we multiply the first equation by $1$, then we will get a $3x$ term that can cancel with the $-3x$ term in the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3x + y &=0 && \\mid \\cdot (1) \\\\ -3x + 5y &=-2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhen we add the two equations, the $x$ terms cancel, and we can solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3x + y &= 0 \\\\\n-3x + 5y &= -2 \\\\\n6y &= (0 - 2) \\\\\n6y &= -2 \\\\\ny &= - \\frac{1}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the first equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3x + (1)\\left(- \\frac{1}{3}\\right) &= 0 \\\\\n3x &= \\frac{1}{3} \\\\\nx &= \\frac{1}{9}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system has *a single solution*, $(\\frac{1}{9},- \\frac{1}{3})$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529316 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "72bba841-9529-5598-b921-f90b8dffbe5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "infinitely many solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529286} {:answer/id #uuid "b27078db-ea72-5bd8-9115-2ea5343e2f7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a single solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529315} {:answer/id #uuid "f96540d7-0eb1-5d77-a5ce-3fa81473a266" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "no solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529331}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b27078db-ea72-5bd8-9115-2ea5343e2f7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a single solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529315} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c1fbec3-c232-5078-a580-2f08b5aa37e3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529282}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c0b2af50-0676-5686-aa21-aed280aaa725" :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-x - 5y &=-12 \\\\ x - 3y &=-2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThis system of equations has {{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Let's start by solving the system using elimination.\n\nNotice that if we multiply the first equation by $1$, then we will get a $-x$ term that can cancel with the $x$ term in the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-x - 5y &=-12 && \\mid \\cdot (1) \\\\ x - 3y &=-2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhen we add the two equations, the $x$ terms cancel, and we can solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-x - 5y &= -12 \\\\\nx - 3y &= -2 \\\\\n-8y &= (-12 - 2) \\\\\n-8y &= -14 \\\\\ny &= \\frac{7}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the first equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-x + (-5)\\left(\\frac{7}{4}\\right) &= -12 \\\\\n-x &= - \\frac{13}{4} \\\\\nx &= \\frac{13}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system has *a single solution*, $(\\frac{13}{4},\\frac{7}{4})$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529318 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8f5d6e30-8e43-5909-9e9a-3a9af82fd1e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a single solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529297} {:answer/id #uuid "9c1af611-f1dc-57a7-b4ff-1cd30b5d58d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "infinitely many solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529300} {:answer/id #uuid "a979b510-0d7a-5573-bce5-5f77d85dffa4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "no solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529310}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8f5d6e30-8e43-5909-9e9a-3a9af82fd1e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a single solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529297} :answer-field/id #uuid "8eaf5b55-2fa9-5c35-a346-66798bb8f61b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529296}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c5970b8b-793c-585b-9c66-c8937f9714fc" :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x - 3y &=-6 \\\\ 4x - y &=-12\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThis system of equations has {{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Let's start by solving the system using elimination.\n\nNotice that if we multiply the first equation by $-4$, then we will get a $-4x$ term that can cancel with the $4x$ term in the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x - 3y &=-6 && \\mid \\cdot (-4) \\\\ 4x - y &=-12\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhen we add the two equations, the $x$ terms cancel, and we can solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-4x + 12y &= 24 \\\\\n4x - y &= -12 \\\\\n11y &= (24 - 12) \\\\\n11y &= 12 \\\\\ny &= \\frac{12}{11}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the first equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx + (-3)\\left(\\frac{12}{11}\\right) &= -6 \\\\\nx &= - \\frac{30}{11} \\\\\nx &= - \\frac{30}{11}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system has *a single solution*, $(- \\frac{30}{11},\\frac{12}{11})$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529418 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "136b59f4-a9e4-5d8c-b869-dad46630033a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529422} {:answer/id #uuid "461e25d2-998f-5231-9ad9-048327e6eb31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529433} {:answer/id #uuid "8ce962ca-5f25-5bd0-8fc8-ea3116721951" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529450} {:answer/id #uuid "90c189d5-a421-5b5e-906b-c9e5138b97ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529452} {:answer/id #uuid "9ab3b094-2f4a-5213-b0de-26f0f8cd39f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529455}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "136b59f4-a9e4-5d8c-b869-dad46630033a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529422} :answer-field/id #uuid "95c3cd81-387f-52f6-8598-4e98964b059c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529453}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "08518a50-98e2-5d9c-a6f5-59dc1a4a2277" :question/problem "A heated metal body cools in a room at ${18}^{∘}\\text{C}.$ Its temperature is ${60}^{∘}\\text{C}$ after $3$ minutes and ${39}^{∘}\\text{C}$ after $7$ minutes. The temperature $T(t)$, in degrees Celsius, obeys Newton's law of cooling, with $t$ measured in minutes since cooling began. To the nearest minute, how long after cooling began does the body reach ${25}^{∘}\\text{C}$?" :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of cooling states that the rate at which the temperature $T(t)$ of a body changes is proportional to the difference between its temperature and its surroundings. Therefore, we have the inhibited decay equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dT}{dt}=-k(T-18),\\qquad k>0.\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dT}{T-18} & =-k\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dT}{T-18} & =-k\\int dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|T-18| & =-kt+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited decay equation, we have $T-18>0$ for all $t>0.$\nNext, we solve for $T(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T-18 & ={e}^{-kt+C} \\\\ T-18 & ={e}^{C}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T-18 & =K{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T & =18+K{e}^{-kt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nApply the two observed conditions $T(3)=60$ and $T(7)=39$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}60 & =18+Ke^{-3k} \\\\ 42 & =Ke^{-3k} \\\\ 39 & =18+Ke^{-7k} \\\\ 21 & =Ke^{-7k}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDivide the excess temperatures to eliminate $K$, then take natural logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{21}{42} & =\\frac{Ke^{-7k}}{Ke^{-3k}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & =e^{-4k} \\\\ \\ln\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) & =-4k \\\\ k & =\\frac{\\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow substitute this rate into $T(3)=60$ to determine the constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}42 & =Ke^{-3\\left(\\frac{\\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}}{4}\\right)} \\\\ K & =42e^{3\\left(\\frac{\\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}}{4}\\right)} \\\\ K & =42\\left(\\frac{42}{21}\\right)^{\\frac{3}{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution satisfying both observations is\n\n$$\nT(t)=18+42\\left(\\frac{42}{21}\\right)^{\\frac{3}{4}}e^{-\\left(\\frac{\\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}}{4}\\right)t}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the two observation times confirms the model:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(3) & =18+(42\\left(\\frac{42}{21}\\right)^{\\frac{3}{4}})e^{-\\left(\\frac{\\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}}{4}\\right)\\cdot 3}=60 \\\\ T(7) & =18+(42\\left(\\frac{42}{21}\\right)^{\\frac{3}{4}})e^{-\\left(\\frac{\\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}}{4}\\right)\\cdot 7}=39\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let ${t}_{1}$ be the time when the body reaches ${25}^{∘}\\text{C}.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({t}_{1}) & =25 \\\\ 18+Ke^{-k{t}_{1}} & =25 \\\\ Ke^{-k{t}_{1}} & =7 \\\\ e^{-k{t}_{1}} & =\\frac{7}{K} \\\\ -k{t}_{1} & =\\ln\\left(\\frac{7}{K}\\right) \\\\ {t}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{k}\\ln\\left(\\frac{7}{K}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute the values of $K$ and $k$ to obtain the time measured since cooling began:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{\\frac{\\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}}{4}}\\ln\\left(\\frac{7}{42\\left(\\frac{42}{21}\\right)^{\\frac{3}{4}}}\\right) \\\\ {t}_{1} & =3+4\\frac{\\ln\\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)}{\\ln\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}≈13\\,\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529434 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2061e414-b4ea-5c57-ad60-fc9a5c241449" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529425} {:answer/id #uuid "3148407a-a1b7-5be3-ae1b-0b7c3252c208" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "19\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529429} {:answer/id #uuid "642227aa-f6b5-5f2c-9789-1258fcc2941b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529439} {:answer/id #uuid "81dae93e-910d-5d6a-839a-efb7b838187c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529447} {:answer/id #uuid "84a07af3-e9fe-57c9-9b86-f82e9bbbe6bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529449}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3148407a-a1b7-5be3-ae1b-0b7c3252c208" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "19\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529429} :answer-field/id #uuid "ab2a34ec-9222-55be-9af3-842e7b4fe46d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529457}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "55811d49-71e0-57b3-9d4a-d1c8d9d4f58f" :question/problem "A heated metal body cools in a room at ${12}^{∘}\\text{C}.$ Its temperature is ${64}^{∘}\\text{C}$ after $1$ minute and ${49}^{∘}\\text{C}$ after $4$ minutes. The temperature $T(t)$, in degrees Celsius, obeys Newton's law of cooling, with $t$ measured in minutes since cooling began. To the nearest minute, how long after cooling began does the body reach ${19}^{∘}\\text{C}$?" :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of cooling states that the rate at which the temperature $T(t)$ of a body changes is proportional to the difference between its temperature and its surroundings. Therefore, we have the inhibited decay equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dT}{dt}=-k(T-12),\\qquad k>0.\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dT}{T-12} & =-k\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dT}{T-12} & =-k\\int dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|T-12| & =-kt+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited decay equation, we have $T-12>0$ for all $t>0.$\nNext, we solve for $T(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T-12 & ={e}^{-kt+C} \\\\ T-12 & ={e}^{C}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T-12 & =K{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T & =12+K{e}^{-kt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nApply the two observed conditions $T(1)=64$ and $T(4)=49$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}64 & =12+Ke^{-1k} \\\\ 52 & =Ke^{-1k} \\\\ 49 & =12+Ke^{-4k} \\\\ 37 & =Ke^{-4k}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDivide the excess temperatures to eliminate $K$, then take natural logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{37}{52} & =\\frac{Ke^{-4k}}{Ke^{-1k}} \\\\ \\frac{37}{52} & =e^{-3k} \\\\ \\ln\\left(\\frac{37}{52}\\right) & =-3k \\\\ k & =- \\frac{\\ln{\\left(\\frac{37}{52} \\right)}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow substitute this rate into $T(1)=64$ to determine the constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}52 & =Ke^{-1\\left(- \\frac{\\ln{\\left(\\frac{37}{52} \\right)}}{3}\\right)} \\\\ K & =52e^{1\\left(- \\frac{\\ln{\\left(\\frac{37}{52} \\right)}}{3}\\right)} \\\\ K & =52\\left(\\frac{52}{37}\\right)^{\\frac{1}{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution satisfying both observations is\n\n$$\nT(t)=12+52\\left(\\frac{52}{37}\\right)^{\\frac{1}{3}}e^{-\\left(- \\frac{\\ln{\\left(\\frac{37}{52} \\right)}}{3}\\right)t}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the two observation times confirms the model:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(1) & =12+(52\\left(\\frac{52}{37}\\right)^{\\frac{1}{3}})e^{-\\left(- \\frac{\\ln{\\left(\\frac{37}{52} \\right)}}{3}\\right)\\cdot 1}=64 \\\\ T(4) & =12+(52\\left(\\frac{52}{37}\\right)^{\\frac{1}{3}})e^{-\\left(- \\frac{\\ln{\\left(\\frac{37}{52} \\right)}}{3}\\right)\\cdot 4}=49\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let ${t}_{1}$ be the time when the body reaches ${19}^{∘}\\text{C}.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({t}_{1}) & =19 \\\\ 12+Ke^{-k{t}_{1}} & =19 \\\\ Ke^{-k{t}_{1}} & =7 \\\\ e^{-k{t}_{1}} & =\\frac{7}{K} \\\\ -k{t}_{1} & =\\ln\\left(\\frac{7}{K}\\right) \\\\ {t}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{k}\\ln\\left(\\frac{7}{K}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute the values of $K$ and $k$ to obtain the time measured since cooling began:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{- \\frac{\\ln{\\left(\\frac{37}{52} \\right)}}{3}}\\ln\\left(\\frac{7}{52\\left(\\frac{52}{37}\\right)^{\\frac{1}{3}}}\\right) \\\\ {t}_{1} & =1+3\\frac{\\ln\\left(\\frac{7}{52}\\right)}{\\ln\\left(\\frac{37}{52}\\right)}≈19\\,\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529436 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "035a9a44-7a16-5905-a0dc-77a5358cf6b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529417} {:answer/id #uuid "09046438-fbef-58e9-b053-5d1dfaf0a2ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529419} {:answer/id #uuid "230162fd-bc9d-51be-85ce-5f37c00f2f06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "58\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529427} {:answer/id #uuid "73a7a8ba-22d0-5b55-b3ed-cf04f2684bc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "28\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529443} {:answer/id #uuid "fcb1a3a5-87a1-5709-994d-fc4337d0f0cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529465}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "09046438-fbef-58e9-b053-5d1dfaf0a2ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529419} :answer-field/id #uuid "21da3e90-7194-505b-be0e-0a0a44ad7031" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529426}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "57bd2c5d-2ec7-5e40-ac6f-231dd6ea513e" :question/problem "A heated metal body cools in a room at ${23}^{∘}\\text{C}.$ Its temperature is ${110}^{∘}\\text{C}$ after $2$ minutes and ${62}^{∘}\\text{C}$ after $8$ minutes. The temperature $T(t)$, in degrees Celsius, obeys Newton's law of cooling, with $t$ measured in minutes since cooling began. To the nearest minute, how long after cooling began does the body reach ${25}^{∘}\\text{C}$?" :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of cooling states that the rate at which the temperature $T(t)$ of a body changes is proportional to the difference between its temperature and its surroundings. Therefore, we have the inhibited decay equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dT}{dt}=-k(T-23),\\qquad k>0.\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dT}{T-23} & =-k\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dT}{T-23} & =-k\\int dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|T-23| & =-kt+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited decay equation, we have $T-23>0$ for all $t>0.$\nNext, we solve for $T(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T-23 & ={e}^{-kt+C} \\\\ T-23 & ={e}^{C}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T-23 & =K{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T & =23+K{e}^{-kt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nApply the two observed conditions $T(2)=110$ and $T(8)=62$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}110 & =23+Ke^{-2k} \\\\ 87 & =Ke^{-2k} \\\\ 62 & =23+Ke^{-8k} \\\\ 39 & =Ke^{-8k}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDivide the excess temperatures to eliminate $K$, then take natural logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{39}{87} & =\\frac{Ke^{-8k}}{Ke^{-2k}} \\\\ \\frac{13}{29} & =e^{-6k} \\\\ \\ln\\left(\\frac{13}{29}\\right) & =-6k \\\\ k & =- \\frac{\\ln{\\left(\\frac{13}{29} \\right)}}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow substitute this rate into $T(2)=110$ to determine the constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}87 & =Ke^{-2\\left(- \\frac{\\ln{\\left(\\frac{13}{29} \\right)}}{6}\\right)} \\\\ K & =87e^{2\\left(- \\frac{\\ln{\\left(\\frac{13}{29} \\right)}}{6}\\right)} \\\\ K & =87\\left(\\frac{87}{39}\\right)^{\\frac{1}{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution satisfying both observations is\n\n$$\nT(t)=23+87\\left(\\frac{87}{39}\\right)^{\\frac{1}{3}}e^{-\\left(- \\frac{\\ln{\\left(\\frac{13}{29} \\right)}}{6}\\right)t}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the two observation times confirms the model:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(2) & =23+(87\\left(\\frac{87}{39}\\right)^{\\frac{1}{3}})e^{-\\left(- \\frac{\\ln{\\left(\\frac{13}{29} \\right)}}{6}\\right)\\cdot 2}=110 \\\\ T(8) & =23+(87\\left(\\frac{87}{39}\\right)^{\\frac{1}{3}})e^{-\\left(- \\frac{\\ln{\\left(\\frac{13}{29} \\right)}}{6}\\right)\\cdot 8}=62\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let ${t}_{1}$ be the time when the body reaches ${25}^{∘}\\text{C}.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({t}_{1}) & =25 \\\\ 23+Ke^{-k{t}_{1}} & =25 \\\\ Ke^{-k{t}_{1}} & =2 \\\\ e^{-k{t}_{1}} & =\\frac{2}{K} \\\\ -k{t}_{1} & =\\ln\\left(\\frac{2}{K}\\right) \\\\ {t}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{k}\\ln\\left(\\frac{2}{K}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute the values of $K$ and $k$ to obtain the time measured since cooling began:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{- \\frac{\\ln{\\left(\\frac{13}{29} \\right)}}{6}}\\ln\\left(\\frac{2}{87\\left(\\frac{87}{39}\\right)^{\\frac{1}{3}}}\\right) \\\\ {t}_{1} & =2+6\\frac{\\ln\\left(\\frac{2}{87}\\right)}{\\ln\\left(\\frac{13}{29}\\right)}≈30\\,\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529438 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "108ed220-ff0d-5a3e-9732-871db25e264d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "42\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529421} {:answer/id #uuid "36b98352-a45c-5756-ba26-e8f13b324484" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "22\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529430} {:answer/id #uuid "40afc4ac-32fd-5122-8320-a1b94bc430b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529431} {:answer/id #uuid "64fd9fd9-a113-5826-9e9f-9bf1153c8645" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "19\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529440} {:answer/id #uuid "751594c1-eba1-5f1c-a604-af02b2e24eff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529444}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "36b98352-a45c-5756-ba26-e8f13b324484" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "22\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529430} :answer-field/id #uuid "f54cefd1-735e-592f-a832-09bdfef2567e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529463}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6178d66f-8125-5a9f-a46c-197579fd4dfe" :question/problem "A heated metal body cools in a room at ${14}^{∘}\\text{C}.$ Its temperature is ${114}^{∘}\\text{C}$ after $2$ minutes and ${79}^{∘}\\text{C}$ after $7$ minutes. The temperature $T(t)$, in degrees Celsius, obeys Newton's law of cooling, with $t$ measured in minutes since cooling began. To the nearest minute, how long after cooling began does the body reach ${32}^{∘}\\text{C}$?" :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of cooling states that the rate at which the temperature $T(t)$ of a body changes is proportional to the difference between its temperature and its surroundings. Therefore, we have the inhibited decay equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dT}{dt}=-k(T-14),\\qquad k>0.\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dT}{T-14} & =-k\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dT}{T-14} & =-k\\int dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|T-14| & =-kt+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited decay equation, we have $T-14>0$ for all $t>0.$\nNext, we solve for $T(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T-14 & ={e}^{-kt+C} \\\\ T-14 & ={e}^{C}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T-14 & =K{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T & =14+K{e}^{-kt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nApply the two observed conditions $T(2)=114$ and $T(7)=79$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}114 & =14+Ke^{-2k} \\\\ 100 & =Ke^{-2k} \\\\ 79 & =14+Ke^{-7k} \\\\ 65 & =Ke^{-7k}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDivide the excess temperatures to eliminate $K$, then take natural logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{65}{100} & =\\frac{Ke^{-7k}}{Ke^{-2k}} \\\\ \\frac{13}{20} & =e^{-5k} \\\\ \\ln\\left(\\frac{13}{20}\\right) & =-5k \\\\ k & =- \\frac{\\ln{\\left(\\frac{13}{20} \\right)}}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow substitute this rate into $T(2)=114$ to determine the constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}100 & =Ke^{-2\\left(- \\frac{\\ln{\\left(\\frac{13}{20} \\right)}}{5}\\right)} \\\\ K & =100e^{2\\left(- \\frac{\\ln{\\left(\\frac{13}{20} \\right)}}{5}\\right)} \\\\ K & =100\\left(\\frac{100}{65}\\right)^{\\frac{2}{5}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution satisfying both observations is\n\n$$\nT(t)=14+100\\left(\\frac{100}{65}\\right)^{\\frac{2}{5}}e^{-\\left(- \\frac{\\ln{\\left(\\frac{13}{20} \\right)}}{5}\\right)t}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the two observation times confirms the model:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(2) & =14+(100\\left(\\frac{100}{65}\\right)^{\\frac{2}{5}})e^{-\\left(- \\frac{\\ln{\\left(\\frac{13}{20} \\right)}}{5}\\right)\\cdot 2}=114 \\\\ T(7) & =14+(100\\left(\\frac{100}{65}\\right)^{\\frac{2}{5}})e^{-\\left(- \\frac{\\ln{\\left(\\frac{13}{20} \\right)}}{5}\\right)\\cdot 7}=79\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let ${t}_{1}$ be the time when the body reaches ${32}^{∘}\\text{C}.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({t}_{1}) & =32 \\\\ 14+Ke^{-k{t}_{1}} & =32 \\\\ Ke^{-k{t}_{1}} & =18 \\\\ e^{-k{t}_{1}} & =\\frac{18}{K} \\\\ -k{t}_{1} & =\\ln\\left(\\frac{18}{K}\\right) \\\\ {t}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{k}\\ln\\left(\\frac{18}{K}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute the values of $K$ and $k$ to obtain the time measured since cooling began:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{- \\frac{\\ln{\\left(\\frac{13}{20} \\right)}}{5}}\\ln\\left(\\frac{18}{100\\left(\\frac{100}{65}\\right)^{\\frac{2}{5}}}\\right) \\\\ {t}_{1} & =2+5\\frac{\\ln\\left(\\frac{9}{50}\\right)}{\\ln\\left(\\frac{13}{20}\\right)}≈22\\,\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529446 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "59b59f9f-0328-5e3a-b3a0-9ae94cd9029b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529437} {:answer/id #uuid "6cc12c7b-7d04-5703-959a-c62329b886f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529442} {:answer/id #uuid "8ed0441f-d7c8-59ac-b964-78a874544707" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529451} {:answer/id #uuid "b6c8bae0-5d37-5021-8cbd-592d9fbcffea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529459} {:answer/id #uuid "c9905343-f98e-5172-a713-e8ad676df9ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "26\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529461}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8ed0441f-d7c8-59ac-b964-78a874544707" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529451} :answer-field/id #uuid "56bac0d5-d078-5477-b2fe-4ca3e2469c85" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529435}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7bca55c5-9e2a-5abf-bde2-2e93390e2953" :question/problem "Simon measures the temperature of the tea in his cup, and the thermometer displays a reading of ${59}^{∘}C.$ After $2$ minutes, Simon repeats the measurement and the thermometer displays a reading of  ${51}^{∘}C.$ To the nearest minute, how long does it take for the tea to decrease its temperature to ${26}^{∘}C$ if the temperature in the room is ${12}^{∘}C?$ \n\n*Hint: You may assume that the temperature $T(t)$ of the tea in Simon's cup decreases at a rate that obeys Newton's law of cooling, where $T$ is measured in degrees Celsius and $t>0$ is the time, in minutes, since the first measurement.*" :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of cooling states that the rate at which the temperature $T(t)$ of a body changes is proportional to the difference between its temperature and its surroundings. Therefore, we have the inhibited decay equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-{T}_{S}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${T}_{S}$ is the temperature of the surroundings, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, ${T}_{S}={12}^{∘}\\text{C},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-12).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dT}{T-12} & =-k\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dT}{T-12} & =-k\\int dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|T-12| & =-kt+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited decay equation, we have $T-12>0$ for all $t>0.$\nNext, we solve for $T(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T-12 & ={e}^{-kt+C} \\\\ T-12 & ={e}^{C}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T-12 & =K{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T & =12+K{e}^{-kt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nWe're told that the initial temperature reading was ${59}^{∘}C.$ So, we apply the initial condition $T(0)=59,$ and solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}59 & =12+K{e}^{0} \\\\ 59 & =12+K \\\\ K & =47\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $T(t):$\n\n$$\nT(t)=12+47{e}^{-kt}\n$$\n\nWe're also told that, after $2$ minutes, the thermometer reading is ${51}^{∘}C.$ So, applying the condition $T(2)=51,$ we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}51 & =12+47{e}^{-2k} \\\\ 39 & =47{e}^{-2k} \\\\ \\frac{39}{47} & ={e}^{-2k} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{39}{47}\\right) & =-2k \\\\ k & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{39}{47}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let ${t}_{1}$ be the time when the temperature of the tea is ${26}^{∘}C.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({t}_{1}) & =26 \\\\ 12+47{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =26 \\\\ 47{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =14 \\\\ {e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =\\frac{14}{47} \\\\ -k{t}_{1} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{14}{47}\\right) \\\\ {t}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{14}{47}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in our value for $k,$ we conclude that the tea will have a temperature of ${26}^{∘}C$ after\n\n$$\n{t}_{1}=\\frac{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{14}{47}\\right)}{-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{39}{47}\\right)}≈13\\,\\text{min},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest minute."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529448 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14340294-f6e6-5843-a6fe-4ec754aa2b3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529423} {:answer/id #uuid "26c1b56e-8363-5b7a-9cd7-fc35a89b4352" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529428} {:answer/id #uuid "42a5f854-c421-5906-9531-dfb338eb3d00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529432} {:answer/id #uuid "68bd5f21-2fc4-57db-ab21-cd5920a10ff8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529441} {:answer/id #uuid "b750d51c-f2a0-5a6c-a1e6-ddae4ed1f957" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529460}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "14340294-f6e6-5843-a6fe-4ec754aa2b3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529423} :answer-field/id #uuid "773705ba-e6bc-585e-ac23-61ef9313aea0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529445}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "83a0fd6e-0040-59af-810e-aa17184cf1d7" :question/problem "The water boils in a pan at a temperature of ${100}^{∘}C.$ The pan with water is then placed to a room with a temperature of ${17}^{∘}C,$ and the temperature of water decreases. After $3$ minutes, it cools to ${83}^{∘}C.$ To the nearest minute, how long does it take for the water to have a temperature of ${43}^{∘}C?$ \n\n*Hint: You may assume that the temperature reading $T(t)$ of the water in the pan decreases at a rate that obeys Newton's law of cooling, where $T$ is measured in degrees Celsius and $t>0$ is the time, in minutes, since the water starts cooling.*" :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of cooling states that the rate at which the temperature $T(t)$ of a body changes is proportional to the difference between its temperature and its surroundings. Therefore, we have the inhibited decay equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-{T}_{S}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${T}_{S}$ is the temperature of the surroundings, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, ${T}_{S}={17}^{∘}\\text{C},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-17).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dT}{T-17} & =-k\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dT}{T-17} & =-k\\int dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|T-17| & =-kt+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited decay equation, we have $T-17>0$ for all $t>0.$\nNext, we solve for $T(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T-17 & ={e}^{-kt+C} \\\\ T-17 & ={e}^{C}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T-17 & =K{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T & =17+K{e}^{-kt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nWe're told that the initial temperature was ${100}^{∘}C.$ So, we apply the initial condition $T(0)=100,$ and solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}100 & =17+K{e}^{0} \\\\ 100 & =17+K \\\\ K & =83\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $T(t):$\n\n$$\nT(t)=17+83{e}^{-kt}\n$$\n\nWe're also told that, after $3$ minutes, it cools to ${83}^{∘}C.$ So, applying the condition $T(3)=83,$ we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}83 & =17+83{e}^{-3k} \\\\ 66 & =83{e}^{-3k} \\\\ \\frac{66}{83} & ={e}^{-3k} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{66}{83}\\right) & =-3k \\\\ k & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{66}{83}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let ${t}_{1}$ be the time when the water has a temperature of ${43}^{∘}C.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({t}_{1}) & =43 \\\\ 17+83{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =43 \\\\ 83{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =26 \\\\ {e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =\\frac{26}{83} \\\\ -k{t}_{1} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{26}{83}\\right) \\\\ {t}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{26}{83}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in our value for $k,$ we conclude that the temperature of water will be ${43}^{∘}C$ after\n\n$$\n{t}_{1}=\\frac{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{26}{83}\\right)}{-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{66}{83}\\right)}≈15\\,\\text{min},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest minute."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529464 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0db77da5-938c-5d21-b72a-0f710e5c2a0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529420} {:answer/id #uuid "1cb8c333-7a33-50bf-877e-90ce08332112" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529424} {:answer/id #uuid "9a43cb5e-136c-5d82-8309-2ec9f8922e29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529454} {:answer/id #uuid "9efca7bd-9559-5f53-b339-6146e7712ff6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529456} {:answer/id #uuid "f372127d-3efe-5b72-a30e-572b7ee9abc2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "17\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529462}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f372127d-3efe-5b72-a30e-572b7ee9abc2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "17\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529462} :answer-field/id #uuid "b4ae2599-3eaa-5407-bab7-c011910aa3c1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529458}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "fa306e16-1691-5350-96e5-61b0e9060130" :question/problem "A heated metal body cools in a room at ${22}^{∘}\\text{C}.$ Its temperature is ${82}^{∘}\\text{C}$ after $2$ minutes and ${54}^{∘}\\text{C}$ after $7$ minutes. The temperature $T(t)$, in degrees Celsius, obeys Newton's law of cooling, with $t$ measured in minutes since cooling began. To the nearest minute, how long after cooling began does the body reach ${31}^{∘}\\text{C}$?" :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of cooling states that the rate at which the temperature $T(t)$ of a body changes is proportional to the difference between its temperature and its surroundings. Therefore, we have the inhibited decay equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dT}{dt}=-k(T-22),\\qquad k>0.\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dT}{T-22} & =-k\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dT}{T-22} & =-k\\int dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|T-22| & =-kt+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited decay equation, we have $T-22>0$ for all $t>0.$\nNext, we solve for $T(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T-22 & ={e}^{-kt+C} \\\\ T-22 & ={e}^{C}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T-22 & =K{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T & =22+K{e}^{-kt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nApply the two observed conditions $T(2)=82$ and $T(7)=54$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}82 & =22+Ke^{-2k} \\\\ 60 & =Ke^{-2k} \\\\ 54 & =22+Ke^{-7k} \\\\ 32 & =Ke^{-7k}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDivide the excess temperatures to eliminate $K$, then take natural logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{32}{60} & =\\frac{Ke^{-7k}}{Ke^{-2k}} \\\\ \\frac{8}{15} & =e^{-5k} \\\\ \\ln\\left(\\frac{8}{15}\\right) & =-5k \\\\ k & =- \\frac{\\ln{\\left(\\frac{8}{15} \\right)}}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow substitute this rate into $T(2)=82$ to determine the constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}60 & =Ke^{-2\\left(- \\frac{\\ln{\\left(\\frac{8}{15} \\right)}}{5}\\right)} \\\\ K & =60e^{2\\left(- \\frac{\\ln{\\left(\\frac{8}{15} \\right)}}{5}\\right)} \\\\ K & =60\\left(\\frac{60}{32}\\right)^{\\frac{2}{5}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution satisfying both observations is\n\n$$\nT(t)=22+60\\left(\\frac{60}{32}\\right)^{\\frac{2}{5}}e^{-\\left(- \\frac{\\ln{\\left(\\frac{8}{15} \\right)}}{5}\\right)t}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the two observation times confirms the model:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(2) & =22+(60\\left(\\frac{60}{32}\\right)^{\\frac{2}{5}})e^{-\\left(- \\frac{\\ln{\\left(\\frac{8}{15} \\right)}}{5}\\right)\\cdot 2}=82 \\\\ T(7) & =22+(60\\left(\\frac{60}{32}\\right)^{\\frac{2}{5}})e^{-\\left(- \\frac{\\ln{\\left(\\frac{8}{15} \\right)}}{5}\\right)\\cdot 7}=54\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let ${t}_{1}$ be the time when the body reaches ${31}^{∘}\\text{C}.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({t}_{1}) & =31 \\\\ 22+Ke^{-k{t}_{1}} & =31 \\\\ Ke^{-k{t}_{1}} & =9 \\\\ e^{-k{t}_{1}} & =\\frac{9}{K} \\\\ -k{t}_{1} & =\\ln\\left(\\frac{9}{K}\\right) \\\\ {t}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{k}\\ln\\left(\\frac{9}{K}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute the values of $K$ and $k$ to obtain the time measured since cooling began:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{- \\frac{\\ln{\\left(\\frac{8}{15} \\right)}}{5}}\\ln\\left(\\frac{9}{60\\left(\\frac{60}{32}\\right)^{\\frac{2}{5}}}\\right) \\\\ {t}_{1} & =2+5\\frac{\\ln\\left(\\frac{3}{20}\\right)}{\\ln\\left(\\frac{8}{15}\\right)}≈17\\,\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530415 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "028fcae0-cbd2-5656-994f-f49bd8d68fde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|{R}_{3}|\\le \\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529582} {:answer/id #uuid "11c234cf-17eb-5252-9439-261d6cb33e3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|{R}_{3}|\\le \\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529612} {:answer/id #uuid "17aeea7d-8357-5924-a3be-cd6d28b250ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|{R}_{3}|\\le \\frac{5}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529626} {:answer/id #uuid "2c6c9f8d-9261-5310-bdc5-6b757360cea7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|{R}_{3}|\\le \\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529675} {:answer/id #uuid "ae8d020f-a077-5090-baab-35bc37c195f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|{R}_{3}|\\le \\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530015}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "17aeea7d-8357-5924-a3be-cd6d28b250ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|{R}_{3}|\\le \\frac{5}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529626} :answer-field/id #uuid "287ab9b4-ef99-51aa-b119-51a1269f75d5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530251}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "009bfd07-1f0d-5688-8e73-36cc8b112d6e" :question/math-academy-id "q-103882" :question/problem "Let $f(x)=\\sqrt{x}$ and let ${P}_{3}(x)$ be the third-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)$ about $x=1.$ Use the Lagrange error bound to find an upper bound for the error when ${P}_{3}(x)$ is used to approximate $\\sqrt{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "The Lagrange error bound states that an upper bound for the error ${R}_{3}$ obtained when using the third-degree Taylor polynomial ${P}_{3}$ to approximate $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n|{R}_{3}|\\le \\frac{M}{4!}|x-1{|}^{4},\n$$\n\nwhere $M$ is the maximum value of $|{f}^{(4)}(x)|$ on the interval between $a=1$ and $x=3.$ \n\nPlugging $x=3$ into the above formula gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{R}_{3}| & \\le \\frac{M}{4!}|3-1{|}^{4} \\\\ |{R}_{3}| & \\le \\frac{M}{24}|2{|}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find $M,$ we need to compute $|{f}^{(4)}(x)|$ and then maximize it on the interval $[1,3].$ Taking the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={x}^{1/2} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-1/2} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{-3/2} \\\\ {f}^{‴}(x) & =\\frac{3}{8}{x}^{-5/2} \\\\ {f}^{(4)}(x) & =-\\frac{15}{16}{x}^{-7/2} \\\\ |{f}^{(4)}(x)| & =|-\\frac{15}{16}{x}^{-7/2}|=\\frac{15}{16}{x}^{-7/2}=\\frac{15}{16{x}^{7/2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $|{f}^{(4)}(x)|=\\frac{15}{16{x}^{7/2}}$ is a decreasing function on the interval $[1,3],$ its value is greatest at $x=1.$ So\n\n$$\nM=|{f}^{(4)}(1)|=\\frac{15}{16(1)^{7/2}}=\\frac{15}{16},\n$$\n\nand therefore, the upper bound is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{R}_{3}| & \\le \\frac{\\left(\\frac{15}{16}\\right)}{24}|2{|}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{15\\cdot {2}^{4}}{16\\cdot 24} \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{24} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530417 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0aa63b29-0409-5b18-83ab-8b4023056556" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{81}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529596} {:answer/id #uuid "7a9c8d26-f55d-57d5-82b3-aed3370cefe8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{36}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529891} {:answer/id #uuid "c72df470-55dc-5ffb-9294-61de19e9549f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{54}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530081} {:answer/id #uuid "cd39717a-740f-51a4-af35-8d0e263f4e78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{36}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530095} {:answer/id #uuid "dd7eacc8-0ca8-5f80-8673-05ce828c5aa5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{144}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530128}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dd7eacc8-0ca8-5f80-8673-05ce828c5aa5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{144}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530128} :answer-field/id #uuid "72b99b6c-e8bf-5475-94ee-9d603ea83a76" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530307}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "04b02bf9-cae6-594f-ac96-340afba92b08" :question/math-academy-id "q-149411" :question/problem "$$\n{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,-1)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{4{y}^{13/4}}, & \\,1\\le y \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables. \nFind the conditional variance $\\text{Var}[Y\\,|\\,X=-1]$ given that the conditional probability density function ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,-1)$ is shown above and the corresponding expected value is ${\\mu }_{Y|-1}=\\frac{9}{5}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the conditional variance of $Y$ given $X=x$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y\\,|\\,X=x] & =\\text{E}[(Y-{\\mu }_{Y|x})^{2}\\,|\\,X=x] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}\\,|\\,X=x]-{\\mu }_{Y|x}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\n\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}\\,|\\,X=x]={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }{y}^{2}\\,{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}\\,|\\,X=-1] & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }{y}^{2}\\,{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,-1)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{y}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{9}{4{y}^{13/4}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{9}{4{y}^{5/4}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{9}{4}{y}^{-5/4}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{9}{4}\\cdot (-4)\\cdot {y}^{-1/4}]}_{1}^{\\infty } \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{9}{\\sqrt[4]{y}}]}_{1}^{\\infty } \\\\  & =0-(-9) \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y\\,|\\,X=-2] & =\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}\\,|\\,X=-2]-{\\mu }_{Y|-2}^{2} \\\\  & =9-{\\left(\\frac{9}{5}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =9-\\frac{81}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{144}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530431 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f341c83-40a1-51c8-a948-eca000c145ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }7{\\left(\\frac{1}{100}\\right)}^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529682} {:answer/id #uuid "4f633459-3d49-5dae-91c2-c677b7f99e15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }7{\\left(\\frac{1}{100}\\right)}^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529771} {:answer/id #uuid "d35c6ff9-930c-5ef8-91d5-af70c973c0a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{7}{\\left(\\frac{1}{10}\\right)}^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530110} {:answer/id #uuid "d4d421d5-51de-5a32-811b-45d752d21a36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{3}{\\left(\\frac{1}{10}\\right)}^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530112} {:answer/id #uuid "de6e0b19-b1eb-5a46-af3b-45dea8fca98f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }7{\\left(\\frac{1}{10}\\right)}^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530135}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "de6e0b19-b1eb-5a46-af3b-45dea8fca98f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }7{\\left(\\frac{1}{10}\\right)}^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530135} :answer-field/id #uuid "62d66ce1-cb4b-511c-ad96-64a90b9a6b51" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530292}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1bafea49-a0df-5bff-bc08-39fb7ee3f6d7" :question/math-academy-id "q-103290" :question/problem "$3.\\overset{―}{7}=$" :question/worked-solution "The given repeating decimal can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3.\\overset{―}{7} & =3.777… \\\\  & =3+0.7+0.07+0.007+… \\\\  & =3+(\\frac{7}{10}+\\frac{7}{100}+\\frac{7}{1\\,000}+…).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series in the parentheses is a geometric series with first term ${a}_{1}=\\frac{7}{10}$ and common ratio $r=\\frac{1}{10}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3.\\overset{―}{7} & =3+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{1}{r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =3+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{7}{10}{\\left(\\frac{1}{10}\\right)}^{n-1} \\\\  & =3+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }7\\left(\\frac{1}{10}\\right){\\left(\\frac{1}{10}\\right)}^{n-1} \\\\  & =3+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }7{\\left(\\frac{1}{10}\\right)}^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530439 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29e9f2a9-81ff-5bf2-8e64-c7bc991f3484" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529670} {:answer/id #uuid "2ceee17a-2287-5109-a8d8-f493be416a9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529677} {:answer/id #uuid "bd62a3ac-b098-5d99-ad5f-adfcc32787b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530052} {:answer/id #uuid "bdcd9ab7-cb20-58e2-8fc7-ff6db6d9d900" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{36}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530054} {:answer/id #uuid "de9d1ae1-ead5-520e-9b15-48bcf7370d81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{18}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530137}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bdcd9ab7-cb20-58e2-8fc7-ff6db6d9d900" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{36}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530054} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa2b95c2-4b35-5103-a6f9-67b22f583604" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530406}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "25e1f7ba-cab0-5f7d-b69c-953924c5cba0" :question/math-academy-id "q-146760" :question/problem "$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3(x+y)}{4}, & 0<x\\le 2y,\\,\\,0<y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}3{y}^{2}, & 0<y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nLet $X$ and $Y$  be continuous random variables. Their joint probability density function and the marginal probability density function of $Y$ are shown above. Find the conditional variance $\\text{Var}[X\\,|\\,Y=1]$ given that the corresponding expected value is ${\\mu }_{X|1}=\\frac{7}{6}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the conditional variance of $X$ given $Y=y$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X\\,|\\,Y=y] & =\\text{E}[(X-{\\mu }_{X|y})^{2}\\,|\\,Y=y] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}\\,|\\,Y=y]-{\\mu }_{X|y}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\n\\text{E}[{X}^{2}\\,|\\,Y=y]={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }{x}^{2}\\,{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\text{E}[X\\,|\\,Y=y]$ is a function of $y.$ \n\nThe conditional density function of $X$ given $Y=y$ can be computed as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y) & =\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{Y}(y)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{3(x+y)}{4}\\right)}{3{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x+y}{4{y}^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0<x\\le 2y,\\,0<y\\le 1.$ Outside this domain, we have ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y)=0.$ \n\nNow, substituting $y=1$ into the expression for ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y),$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,1)=\\frac{x+1}{4}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}\\,|\\,Y=1]{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }{x}^{2}\\,{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{(x+1)}{4}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}({x}^{3}+{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{[\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(4+\\frac{8}{3})-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\left(\\frac{20}{3}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X\\,|\\,Y=1] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}\\,|\\,Y=1]-{\\mu }_{X|1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}-{\\left(\\frac{7}{6}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}-\\frac{49}{36} \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{36}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530440 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5db52af3-2d00-57fe-83ac-e1e4cabb425b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529806} {:answer/id #uuid "6716a6d3-11e8-5185-8a14-c81cf63f513b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{18}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529837} {:answer/id #uuid "a5022d84-3876-5770-bcec-f22b051278c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529988} {:answer/id #uuid "cde47949-e5a4-500e-82a8-40c11888b0e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{36}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530097} {:answer/id #uuid "d6596022-5d1b-577f-a508-e5cb0a327c6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530115}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6716a6d3-11e8-5185-8a14-c81cf63f513b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{18}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529837} :answer-field/id #uuid "7186124f-248a-5608-b06b-90a979a094ca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530305}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2866f3dd-6eaf-53a1-aed5-f4ad68a3ab1f" :question/math-academy-id "q-146395" :question/problem "$$\n{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,1)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2y, & \\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables. \nFind the conditional variance $\\text{Var}[Y\\,|\\,X=1]$ given that the conditional probability density function ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,1)$ is shown above and the corresponding expected value is ${\\mu }_{Y|1}=\\frac{2}{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the conditional variance of $Y$ given $X=x$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y\\,|\\,X=x] & =\\text{E}[(Y-{\\mu }_{Y|x})^{2}\\,|\\,X=x] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}\\,|\\,X=x]-{\\mu }_{Y|x}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\n\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}\\,|\\,X=x]={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }{y}^{2}\\,{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}\\,|\\,X=1] & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }{y}^{2}\\,{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,1)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{2}\\cdot 2y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{2{y}^{4}}{4}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y\\,|\\,X=1] & =\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}\\,|\\,X=1]-{\\mu }_{Y|1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}-{\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{4}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{18}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530444 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f185006-9954-5514-b577-4d8238b5f3a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529646} {:answer/id #uuid "3bd874c7-e528-5b1f-9ca7-6d9dd97ff023" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{160}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529720} {:answer/id #uuid "48b37456-2b35-5ad7-b4cb-c1cd5bcf7bc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{160}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529750} {:answer/id #uuid "eda5a28b-0e86-5d28-8ea2-d7a1062c5778" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{40}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530175} {:answer/id #uuid "fbf07cfd-45b2-51de-9143-b4f7db4de0be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{19}{320}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530205}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fbf07cfd-45b2-51de-9143-b4f7db4de0be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{19}{320}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530205} :answer-field/id #uuid "61c27587-c036-5512-931c-7ba163299613" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530291}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "37a3fb25-8245-5c86-91e5-a1e534022eb1" :question/math-academy-id "q-146976" :question/problem "$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}3x(1-{y}^{2}), & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2x, & 0\\le x\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ and $Y$  be continuous random variables. Their joint probability density function and the marginal probability density function of $X$ are shown above. Find the conditional variance $\\text{Var}[Y\\,|\\,X=1]$ given that the corresponding expected value is ${\\mu }_{Y|1}=\\frac{3}{8}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the conditional variance of $Y$ given $X=x$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y\\,|\\,X=x] & =\\text{E}[(Y-{\\mu }_{Y|x})^{2}\\,|\\,X=x] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}\\,|\\,X=x]-{\\mu }_{Y|x}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\n\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}\\,|\\,X=x]={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }{y}^{2}\\,{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\text{E}[Y\\,|\\,X=x]$ is a function of $x.$ \n\nThe conditional density function of $Y$ given $X=x$ can be computed as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x) & =\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{X}(x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3x(1-{y}^{2})}{2x} \\\\  & =\\frac{3(1-{y}^{2})}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1.$ Outside this domain, we have ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)=0.$\n\nNotice that the factor of $x$ cancels out, leaving an expression that does not depend on $x.$ Therefore, substituting $x=1$ into the expression for ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)$ has no effect:\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,1)=\\frac{3(1-{y}^{2})}{2}\n$$\n\nThen, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}\\,|\\,X=1] & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }{y}^{2}\\,{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,1)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{3(1-{y}^{2})}{2}\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({y}^{2}-{y}^{4})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}[\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{{y}^{5}}{5}]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}[(\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{5})-0] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y\\,|\\,X=1] & =\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}\\,|\\,X=1]-{\\mu }_{Y|1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}-{\\left(\\frac{3}{8}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}-\\frac{9}{64} \\\\  & =\\frac{19}{320}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530445 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7382e9bb-66e3-536f-bf47-96a851f09b48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{384}{49}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529874} {:answer/id #uuid "88e834ca-6b87-553a-a237-a4681d65ed06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{381}{49}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529912} {:answer/id #uuid "a56625c4-5e98-548c-bea6-5d94c2fb908e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{54}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529992} {:answer/id #uuid "cb89ee2c-2a49-5be9-9e3b-ad09f4a34f3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{51}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530091} {:answer/id #uuid "e6cbeb1a-1375-5455-b8f6-f5ce904eaab8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{360}{49}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530157}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a56625c4-5e98-548c-bea6-5d94c2fb908e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{54}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529992} :answer-field/id #uuid "de48ce71-ed4e-5a50-80fd-461f4a5c82ff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530383}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3b4c1a29-e4bc-58eb-ae8a-170f49cecb2b" :question/math-academy-id "q-146761" :question/problem "$$\n{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,0)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{14}\\sqrt{x}, & \\,1\\le x\\le 4 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables. \nFind the conditional variance $\\text{Var}[X\\,|\\,Y=0]$ given that the conditional probability density function ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,0)$ is shown above and the corresponding expected value is ${\\mu }_{X|0}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{7}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the conditional variance of $X$ given $Y=y$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X\\,|\\,Y=y] & =\\text{E}[(X-{\\mu }_{X|y})^{2}\\,|\\,Y=y] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}\\,|\\,Y=y]-{\\mu }_{X|y}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\n\\text{E}[{X}^{2}\\,|\\,Y=y]={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }{x}^{2}\\,{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}\\,|\\,Y=0] & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }{x}^{2}\\,{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,0)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{3}{14}\\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{3}{14}{x}^{5/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{3}{49}{x}^{7/2}]}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{49}(128-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{381}{49}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X\\,|\\,Y=0] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}\\,|\\,Y=0]-{\\mu }_{X|0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{381}{49}-{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{7}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{381}{49}-\\frac{3}{49} \\\\  & =\\frac{378}{49} \\\\  & =\\frac{54}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530446 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2bbe50e9-7976-59a7-89c3-05577c4d4e69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|{R}_{2}|\\le 0.012" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529673} {:answer/id #uuid "40a27b19-7d0a-5731-a5f5-80e7d90ea7b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|{R}_{2}|\\le 0.031" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529732} {:answer/id #uuid "79fa3435-6ee8-5d6f-8f2d-f21bcdc60b02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|{R}_{2}|\\le 0.123" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529890} {:answer/id #uuid "99d6488f-dc3c-50ed-ba38-298b99e1c11a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|{R}_{2}|\\le 0.313" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529957} {:answer/id #uuid "adaee6c3-bba1-5f68-9ad5-e21e92e1228b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|{R}_{2}|\\le 0.215" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530011}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "99d6488f-dc3c-50ed-ba38-298b99e1c11a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|{R}_{2}|\\le 0.313" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529957} :answer-field/id #uuid "4166e2b7-c9ea-52ad-b2a3-8d0fb32213b3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530263}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3d9e0c2d-cfb3-5637-bf5c-58e8b1c970c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-103872" :question/problem "Let $f(x)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}$ and let ${P}_{2}(x)$ be the second-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)$ about $x=1.$ Use the Lagrange error bound to find an upper bound for the error when ${P}_{2}(x)$ is used to approximate $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}.$ Round your answer to three decimal places." :question/worked-solution "The Lagrange error bound states that an upper bound for the error ${R}_{2}$ obtained when using the second-degree Taylor polynomial ${P}_{2}$ to approximate $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n|{R}_{2}|\\le \\frac{M}{3!}|x-1{|}^{3},\n$$\n\nwhere $M$ is the maximum value of $|{f}^{(3)}(x)|$ on the interval between $a=1$ and $x=2.$ \n\nPlugging $x=2$ into the above formula gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{R}_{2}| & \\le \\frac{M}{3!}|2-1{|}^{3} \\\\ |{R}_{2}| & \\le \\frac{M}{6}|1{|}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find $M,$ we need to compute $|{f}^{(3)}(x)|$ and then maximize it on the interval $[1,2].$ Taking the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={x}^{-1/2} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-3/2} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =\\frac{3}{4}{x}^{-5/2} \\\\ {f}^{(3)}(x) & =-\\frac{15}{8}{x}^{-7/2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{15}{8{x}^{7/2}} \\\\ |{f}^{(3)}(x)| & =|-\\frac{15}{8{x}^{7/2}}|=\\frac{15}{8{x}^{7/2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $|{f}^{(3)}(x)|=\\frac{15}{8{x}^{7/2}}$ is a decreasing function on the interval $[1,2],$ its value is greatest at $x=1.$ So\n\n$$\nM=|{f}^{(3)}(1)|=\\frac{15}{8(1)^{7/2}}=\\frac{15}{8},\n$$\n\nand therefore, the upper bound is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{R}_{2}| & \\le \\frac{\\left(\\frac{15}{8}\\right)}{6}|1{|}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{16} \\\\  & ≈0.313\\,,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nup to three decimal places."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530448 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e1b3266-8abd-5a10-871e-4abc1fdc45fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{144}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529642} {:answer/id #uuid "70cf3fbb-1f23-5477-a624-77b52dc5dfa8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529868} {:answer/id #uuid "83567d3e-a18e-59f5-8de5-33ca7b6f14ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529906} {:answer/id #uuid "8f5d62b3-e68e-5f3b-a602-8ee58debbe22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{144}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529928} {:answer/id #uuid "c6bcefcf-5fab-5be9-97ba-7dd8b81b7c3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530077}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8f5d62b3-e68e-5f3b-a602-8ee58debbe22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{144}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529928} :answer-field/id #uuid "f6856b8d-546d-52e0-bed7-386f3bdb5d0b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530400}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "48276d5d-5662-5546-a559-258dff238b59" :question/math-academy-id "q-147052" :question/problem "$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}x-y, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,-1\\le y\\le 0, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}x+\\frac{1}{2}, & 0\\le x\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ and $Y$  be continuous random variables. Their joint probability density function and the marginal probability density function of $X$ are shown above. Find the conditional variance $\\text{Var}[Y\\,|\\,X=\\frac{1}{2}]$ given that the corresponding expected value is ${\\mu }_{Y|\\frac{1}{2}}=-\\frac{7}{12}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the conditional variance of $Y$ given $X=x$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y\\,|\\,X=x] & =\\text{E}[(Y-{\\mu }_{Y|x})^{2}\\,|\\,X=x] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}\\,|\\,X=x]-{\\mu }_{Y|x}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\n\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}\\,|\\,X=x]={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }{y}^{2}\\,{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\text{E}[Y\\,|\\,X=x]$ is a function of $x.$ \n\nThe conditional density function of $Y$ given $X=x$ can be computed as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x) & =\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{X}(x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{x-y}{(x+\\frac{1}{2})},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le x\\le 1,\\,-1\\le y\\le 0.$ Outside this domain, we have ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)=0.$\n\nNow, substituting $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ into the expression for ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x),$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,\\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{(\\frac{1}{2}-y)}{(\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2})}=\\frac{1}{2}-y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}\\,|\\,X=\\frac{1}{2}] & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }{y}^{2}\\,{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,\\frac{1}{2})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{y}^{2}(\\frac{1}{2}-y)\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}-{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{y}^{3}}{6}-\\frac{{y}^{4}}{4}]}_{-1}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}+\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y\\,|\\,X=\\frac{1}{2}] & =\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}\\,|\\,X=\\frac{1}{2}]-{\\mu }_{Y|\\frac{1}{2}}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{12}-{\\left(-\\frac{7}{12}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{12}-\\frac{49}{144} \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{144}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530450 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2aa10fa5-bc74-53cb-82c6-c292399809ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529671}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2aa10fa5-bc74-53cb-82c6-c292399809ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529671} :answer-field/id #uuid "f9008fa5-bcba-5a22-b50b-b30fa83037e9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530403}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "48d3004f-21c4-5fdb-bf3a-f534994324c2" :question/math-academy-id "q-351678" :question/problem "Find the radius of convergence $R$ for the series\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{n}^{4}(3x-3)^{n}=(3x-3)+{2}^{4}(3x-3)^{2}+{3}^{4}(3x-3)^{3}+⋯\\,.\n$$\n\n$R=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To apply the ratio test, we define ${a}_{n}={n}^{4}(3x-3)^{n}.$ Then, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(n+1)^{4}(3x-3)^{n+1}}{{n}^{4}(3x-3)^{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(n+1)^{4}(3x-3)}{{n}^{4}}| \\\\  & =|3x-3|{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\left(\\frac{n+1}{n}\\right)}^{4} \\\\  & =|3x-3|\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =3|x-1|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L<1.$ In this case, the convergence condition is satisfied if $|x-1|<\\frac{1}{3}.$ Therefore, we conclude that the radius of convergence is $R=\\frac{1}{3}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530453 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5f9e5a1d-3f4a-5463-86bd-0a7d49d5ac4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "300" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529810}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5f9e5a1d-3f4a-5463-86bd-0a7d49d5ac4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "300" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529810} :answer-field/id #uuid "042655a5-af48-5ad5-8a8e-790078669a6e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530217} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9ffdcfd8-bb52-5ed3-b6b0-c9aba03c3e85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{100}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529976}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9ffdcfd8-bb52-5ed3-b6b0-c9aba03c3e85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{100}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529976} :answer-field/id #uuid "34435d1f-0b9d-51d1-a200-90903e9fb178" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530260}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4eef4f5e-6db7-5b25-85cf-e831027c6756" :question/math-academy-id "q-351710" :question/problem "Express $3.\\overset{―}{03}$ as a geometric series.\n\n$3.\\overset{―}{03}={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }$\n{{field-1}}$($\n{{field-2}}$)^{n}$" :question/worked-solution "The given repeating decimal can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3.\\overset{―}{03} & =3.030\\,303… \\\\  & =3+0.03+0.000\\,3+0.000\\,003+… \\\\  & =\\underbrace{3+\\frac{3}{100}+\\frac{3}{10\\,000}+\\frac{3}{1\\,000\\,000}+⋯.}_{\\text{geometric series}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that we have a geometric series where the first term is ${a}_{1}=3$ and the common ratio is $\\frac{1}{100}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3.\\overset{―}{03} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{1}{r}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }3{\\left(\\frac{1}{100}\\right)}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }300\\left(\\frac{1}{100}\\right){\\left(\\frac{1}{100}\\right)}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }300{\\left(\\frac{1}{100}\\right)}^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530454 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f3256b67-7e99-5826-a95a-b55a197e4d06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530184}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f3256b67-7e99-5826-a95a-b55a197e4d06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530184} :answer-field/id #uuid "12fa28de-d131-50b4-b0e1-7dd696b0e54f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530230}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4f72f786-eb31-5b03-9051-ae70858e9f5f" :question/math-academy-id "q-352035" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx={t}^{2},\\,y=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}{t}^{4},\\,t\\in [0,\\sqrt[4]{6}].\n$$\n\nDetermine the surface area $S$ generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis. Do not round the answer.\n\n$S=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=0$ to ${t}_{2}=\\sqrt[4]{6}.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})=2t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}{t}^{4})=\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{3}}{t}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[4]{6}}{t}^{2}\\sqrt{(2t)^{2}+{(\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{3}}{t}^{3})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[4]{6}}{t}^{2}\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+\\frac{16}{3}{t}^{6}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[4]{6}}{t}^{2}\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}(1+\\frac{4}{3}{t}^{4})}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[4]{6}}{t}^{2}\\cdot 2|t|\\sqrt{1+\\frac{4}{3}{t}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[4]{6}}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{4}{3}{t}^{4}}\\cdot {t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the last integral using the substitution $u=1+\\frac{4}{3}{t}^{4}.$ Then, $\\text{d}u=\\frac{16}{3}{t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t⟹{t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t=\\frac{3}{16}\\text{d}u.$ We also update the limits of integration for $u:$\n\n| **$t$** | $0$ | $\\sqrt[4]{6}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $9$ |\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[4]{6}}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{4}{3}{t}^{4}}\\cdot {t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{9}\\sqrt{u}\\cdot \\frac{3}{16}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}{\\int }_{1}^{9}{u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\cdot {[\\frac{2}{3}{u}^{3/2}]}_{1}^{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}[(9)^{3/2}-(1)^{3/2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}(27-1) \\\\  & =13\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530457 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6878ad2d-c3f6-5472-b932-d7a4364f9bc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi \\operatorname{ln}⁡2{\\int }_{0}^{3}{4}^{t}\\sqrt{{t}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529841} {:answer/id #uuid "735f35d2-93c2-528a-acc9-d6a1cf48503f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}{t}^{2}\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+{4}^{t}{ln}^{2}⁡2}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529872} {:answer/id #uuid "8f18c374-0ec5-5b5a-8aa2-bf73f6a415be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi \\operatorname{ln}⁡2{\\int }_{0}^{3}{2}^{t}\\sqrt{{2}^{t}+1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529926} {:answer/id #uuid "b4866bd0-791b-5423-befb-168b1ad8cf39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}{2}^{t}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+{2}^{t}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530030} {:answer/id #uuid "cb3c2df3-9d88-53e0-973d-199d14e0ed36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi \\operatorname{ln}⁡2{\\int }_{0}^{3}{t}^{4}{2}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530090}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "735f35d2-93c2-528a-acc9-d6a1cf48503f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}{t}^{2}\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+{4}^{t}{ln}^{2}⁡2}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529872} :answer-field/id #uuid "b4a256d7-74ef-5f59-aa17-d233ba137369" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530348}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5349d9de-8c6d-5416-a022-6878977b6004" :question/math-academy-id "q-139057" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx={t}^{2},\\,y={2}^{t},\\,t\\in [0,3].\n$$\n\nWhat integral gives the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=0$ to ${t}_{2}=3.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})=2t \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({2}^{t})={2}^{t}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}{t}^{2}\\sqrt{{(2t)}^{2}+{({2}^{t}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}{t}^{2}\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+{2}^{2t}{ln}^{2}⁡2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}{t}^{2}\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+{4}^{t}{ln}^{2}⁡2}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530459 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23beb7dd-cb27-516a-87c2-22187aecddb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{12}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529656} {:answer/id #uuid "694824b2-68f4-5131-a788-8727d4006841" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{24}{49}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529847} {:answer/id #uuid "98abe252-a017-5e77-b742-868d2adf14cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{24}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529954} {:answer/id #uuid "a16fd62d-5444-5e85-96f9-c5d6c7412063" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{12}{49}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529980} {:answer/id #uuid "b3d991b4-30f6-52a1-afce-0bb0222c6615" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{48}{49}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530028}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "694824b2-68f4-5131-a788-8727d4006841" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{24}{49}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529847} :answer-field/id #uuid "dccf4473-8ce6-5a7b-9170-9d177e05cbfd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530381}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "59aca6d9-1752-57b6-a727-2d8a95f6602b" :question/math-academy-id "q-149357" :question/problem "$$\n{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,0)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{12}{7{x}^{5/2}}, & \\,1\\le x\\le 4 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables. \nFind the conditional variance $\\text{Var}[X\\,|\\,Y=0]$ given that the conditional probability density function ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,0)$ is shown above and the corresponding expected value is ${\\mu }_{X|0}=\\frac{12}{7}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the conditional variance of $X$ given $Y=y$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X\\,|\\,Y=y] & =\\text{E}[(X-{\\mu }_{X|y})^{2}\\,|\\,Y=y] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}\\,|\\,Y=y]-{\\mu }_{X|y}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\n\\text{E}[{X}^{2}\\,|\\,Y=y]={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }{x}^{2}\\,{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}\\,|\\,Y=0] & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }{x}^{2}\\,{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,0)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{12}{7{x}^{5/2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{12}{7\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{24\\sqrt{x}}{7}]}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{48}{7}-\\frac{24}{7} \\\\  & =\\frac{24}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X\\,|\\,Y=0] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}\\,|\\,Y=0]-{\\mu }_{X|0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{24}{7}-{\\left(\\frac{12}{7}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{24}{7}-\\frac{144}{49} \\\\  & =\\frac{24}{49}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530464 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34cc357a-5d15-5858-a9da-1fbed5a44025" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{21}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529702} {:answer/id #uuid "5e7acdc3-b79c-540c-8876-d8d4ab0dc5e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529807} {:answer/id #uuid "6726955f-fcbe-51ef-81c8-c5b97a0a797b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529838} {:answer/id #uuid "78434c08-58fd-5864-98a8-e08d7e0b4b36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529886} {:answer/id #uuid "dda5e757-29ae-51d9-ac4e-1c47bef47985" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530129}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dda5e757-29ae-51d9-ac4e-1c47bef47985" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530129} :answer-field/id #uuid "06e3e1ec-1b29-5e88-912e-1d47aa3d4d7d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530221}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6104314b-b213-5403-b96a-2e0ddf20d4ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-149399" :question/problem "$$\n{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,5)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{{x}^{4}}, & \\,x\\le -1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables. \nFind the conditional variance $\\text{Var}[X\\,|\\,Y=5]$ given that the conditional probability density function ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,5)$ is shown above and the corresponding expected value is ${\\mu }_{X|5}=-\\frac{3}{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the conditional variance of $X$ given $Y=y$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X\\,|\\,Y=y] & =\\text{E}[(X-{\\mu }_{X|y})^{2}\\,|\\,Y=y] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}\\,|\\,Y=y]-{\\mu }_{X|y}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\n\\text{E}[{X}^{2}\\,|\\,Y=y]={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }{x}^{2}\\,{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}\\,|\\,Y=5] & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }{x}^{2}\\,{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,5)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{3}{{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}3{x}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-3\\cdot {x}^{-1}]}_{-\\infty }^{-1} \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{3}{x}]}_{-\\infty }^{-1} \\\\  & =3-0 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X\\,|\\,Y=5] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}\\,|\\,Y=5]-{\\mu }_{X|5}^{2} \\\\  & =3-{\\left(-\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =3-\\frac{9}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530465 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "961eb0d5-8253-50e6-a961-755f2db89486" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7\\pi}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529944}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "961eb0d5-8253-50e6-a961-755f2db89486" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7\\pi}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529944} :answer-field/id #uuid "a3f06b1d-08c6-50bc-8914-e903872c2d7d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530333}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6528b016-7e7a-5f42-bb80-0fc7063586be" :question/math-academy-id "q-352031" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{2}t,\\,y=\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2},\\,t\\in [0,\\sqrt{3}].\n$$\n\nDetermine the surface area $S$ generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis. Do not round the answer.\n\n$S=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=0$ to ${t}_{2}=\\sqrt{3}.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{2}t)=\\frac{1}{2}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2})=\\frac{1}{2}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{1}{2}t\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{2}+{(\\frac{1}{2}t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{1}{2}t\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{1}{2}t\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}(1+{t}^{2})}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{1}{2}t\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}\\cdot 2t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the last integral using the substitution $u=1+{t}^{2}.$ Then, $\\text{d}u=2t\\,\\text{d}t.$ We also update the limits of integration for $u:$\n\n| **$t$** | $0$ | $\\sqrt{3}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $4$ |\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}\\cdot 2t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}{\\int }_{1}^{4}{u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\cdot {[\\frac{2}{3}{u}^{3/2}]}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}[(4)^{3/2}-(1)^{3/2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}(8-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530470 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9b723d11-d6c7-5d3e-8fea-3e5b98c838ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529964}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9b723d11-d6c7-5d3e-8fea-3e5b98c838ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529964} :answer-field/id #uuid "2377874d-27f0-5164-a2c7-cb6157512b5a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530244} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "46779c75-efd5-5aa6-aa17-5ae0d3ead20c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{100}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529744}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "46779c75-efd5-5aa6-aa17-5ae0d3ead20c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{100}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529744} :answer-field/id #uuid "5265f3e8-35f3-5e0a-b6c5-f7c70a42a39d" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530278} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "286b2cc1-30b8-53ae-a45a-2c71feadf867" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529667}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "286b2cc1-30b8-53ae-a45a-2c71feadf867" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529667} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa6ce5eb-1dda-5885-86ed-25c5a53741a0" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530408}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6baf96f3-d002-5d3c-bd1a-08275622d74d" :question/math-academy-id "q-351736" :question/problem "Express $0.4\\overset{―}{25}$ using a geometric series.\n\n$0.4\\overset{―}{25}=$\n{{field-1}}\n$+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }$\n{{field-2}}$(${{field-3}}$)^{n}$" :question/worked-solution "The given repeating decimal can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.4\\overset{―}{25} & =0.425\\,252\\,5… \\\\  & =0.4+0.025+0.000\\,25+0.000\\,002\\,5+… \\\\  & =0.4+(\\frac{25}{1\\,000}+\\frac{25}{100\\,000}+\\frac{25}{10\\,000\\,000}+…).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series in the parentheses is a geometric series with first term ${a}_{1}=\\frac{25}{1\\,000}$ and common ratio $r=\\frac{1}{100}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.4\\overset{―}{25} & =0.4+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{1}{r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =0.4+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{25}{1\\,000}{\\left(\\frac{1}{100}\\right)}^{n-1} \\\\  & =0.4+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{25}{10}\\left(\\frac{1}{100}\\right){\\left(\\frac{1}{100}\\right)}^{n-1} \\\\  & =0.4+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }2.5{\\left(\\frac{1}{100}\\right)}^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]
